  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 946 PM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nISOLATED RAIN SHOWERS AND THUNDERSTORMS NEXT COUPLE DAYS:\n\nDeterministic models continue to push a shortwave through the \nregion over the next 48 or so hours. With moisture still sitting\nabove normal, this added forcing will promote an uptick in \nstorm coverage across the high terrain Tuesday and Wednesday \nafternoon. However, this added support looks too weak to prompt \nmany stronger storms. Regardless, portions of the San Juans and\ncentral Colorado mountains could see isolated wetting rains \nover the next couple day. Valleys should stay dry. The shortwave\nquickly dissipates by Thursday, leaving us underneath very weak\nflow that will only serve to continue very sparse showers over \nthe mountains like we've seen the past couple days. Otherwise, \nwe are still running hotter than normal, up to 15 degrees above \nnormal in places, and this trend should persist through the \nweek.\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES INCREASE EARLY NEXT WEEK:\n\nWe remain confident in a pattern shift late in the weekend or \nearly next work week as a closed low is forecast to drop down \nfrom the PacNW. However, we have lost a little bit in confidence\non the timing of the greatest surge of moisture, ranging \nanytime from early Monday morning to Tuesday afternoon. As it \nstands, the highest probability for the strongest push of \nmoisture (and resulting rainfall) continues to be Monday \nafternoon and into the overnight hours, but the lack of \nconsistency across several forecast runs is keeping clarity of \nthe finer details at a minimum. Regardless, it seems very likely\nat this point that we are on the verge of another round of more\nwidespread precipitation in spite of the uncertainty regarding \ntiming, location, and strength. Keep an eye on the latest \nforecast over the coming days for additional updates as we keep \nawaiting better agreement among models.",
